Z||000000||Smoooooth GT||09-16-2001||10:43 AM||||when I open up the 4 barrel going down the road, it bogg's down(foot still on floor)and will choke itself down?? what's wrong with it??  it has the vac adv. that goes to the dist. ....  I don't have a clue..  help me please    steve  <P>ps ..  any clues will help...    <P>------------------<BR>NOS Injected 2.8L<BR>Future add's.. 2" drop, 17" wheel's, finish the turbo T-3watercooled project, ViperYellow <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smooth_sig2_4.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula||09-16-2001||11:25 AM||||my fiero does that too..... (4 bbl too)..<P><BR>any help would be appreciated from carb guys!<P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B><I>STROKE THIS!</FONT></FONT></B></I>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Flag.jpg">    <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Formulasiggy.jpg">    <BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B><I>FORMULA</FONT></FONT></B></I>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||09-16-2001||01:41 PM||||Has it always done that? Sounds like there's not enough gas for the amount of air that's geting in. What kind / size carb is it?<BR>If the carb is too big for the engine, this can happen. <I>Especially</I> if it's a Holley carb with mechanical secondaries. (Vacuum secondaries are much better for street cars) Carter AFBs, Edelbrock, and Rochester Quadrajet carbs with the air valve operated secondaries are much more forgiving. <BR>Another possibility is that your fuel pump can't keep up with the carb. I had a Carter Thermo Quad on my 350 Monza for a while, and it would actually "outrun" the fuel pump. <BR>Engine would fall flat on it's face. Just like a rev limiter. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 09-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||09-16-2001||03:43 PM||||If it is a Quadrajet, the secondary fuel pickups have probably fallen off. Remove the top of the carb and look to see if they are still attached to it, if not they need to be pressed back in. Also carbs need something like 7 psi fuel pressure at idle and at least 4 at full throttle.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Smoooooth GT||09-16-2001||10:37 PM||||thank's everybody...  It does'nt say what kind or size it is... how can I tell ?? Thank's again...  Steve||||reg||1
Z||000005||California Kid||09-16-2001||10:55 PM||||Carbs have to matched to what the engine will flow air wise. Generally speaking your camshaft, intake manifold, heads, and exhaust will dictate the size Carb and jet sizes, metering rods, power valves, etc. If you don't understand exactly how it works, pay someone to set it up or suggest the proper carb for you. It shouldn't bog at all if it's set properly. Carbs can be very complicated if you don't understand how they work, you won't solve this on the forum, as the whole setup has to be looked at if you want to get the most out of it.<P>If it's a crate motor or un-altered stock engine, most good speed shops should be able able to tell you if you have the right carb or recommend proper jets and metering rods for your setup. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula||09-16-2001||11:16 PM||||the guy who built my engine was making 270 hp out of it with a edelbrock 390.  And I put a holley 390 on it and it doesnt want to pull all the way to 7 grand.  it starts bogging around 4 then I let off.  I actually think its my ignition.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Archie||09-16-2001||11:51 PM||||Sounds like ignition to me too. Do you still have a functioning o2 sensor on it?<P>Archie<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||falconhulk||09-17-2001||12:37 PM||||This is definitely a to much fuel problem.  The secondary are opening to soon.  Vacuum secondary are the best as opposed to mechanical secondary or double pumpers.  This is a common problem in carbed cars.  I have a carbed mustang that I went from a Holley mech. to a carter vacuum and it fixed the problem.  The secondary don't open unless they are needed.  For normal driving the car behaves itself.  When you stomp it the beast awakens!!  For vacuum secondary the engine has to be under load and you cannot open them by just stomping on the gas with it in park.  With mech secondary they will open and screw up the mixture.  If you have mech secondary and want to stay with them even rejetting may not help.  You may have to go to a smaller carb.  If you have a vac secondary then rejetting will definitely help.  I would suggest going with a vacuum carb for any performance/street car.<p>[This message has been edited by falconhulk (edited 09-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Formula||09-17-2001||01:05 PM||||the O2 sensor works but I dont think the computer is controlling anything anymore (carbed with vacuum advance)..<P>It has vacuum secondaries that dont open when it is in park being revved up.  I thought that was the problem for a while but I guess not.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Formula88||09-17-2001||01:57 PM||||Ok, my carb knowledge is limited to Quadrajets, so I'll talk about them....<P>Bog when you floor it means the secondaries are opening too fast - you're flooding the engine.  On a quadrabog, spring tension and vacuum control the secondary opening rate.  You adjust the spring tension on the secondary butterflies until it's tight enough to not bog.  You want to loosen them up until it bogs, then tighten it just enough to keep it from bogging.  That gives you the best performance.  If it's tighter, they're not opening all the way, or as fast as they could.<P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><I><FONT COLOR="blue">The path of the righteous man is beset on all sides by the inequities of the selfish and the tyrranys of evil men. Blessed is he who in the name of charity and good will shepards the weak through the valley of darkness for he is truly his brothers keeper, and the finder of lost children. And I shall strike down upon thee with great vengence and furious anger, those who attempt to poison and destroy my brothers. And you will know my name is the lord when I lay my vengence upon thee. </I></FONT></FONT><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/9-11-01a.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000011||falconhulk||09-17-2001||02:52 PM||||Exactly formula 88.  This should be a pretty easy problem to solve.  good luck.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/i-4_b_l.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Archie||09-18-2001||01:27 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula:<BR><B>the O2 sensor works but I dont think the computer is controlling anything anymore (carbed with vacuum advance)..<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well I'm assuming that you've already checked into overcarburation as being the cause of it bogging down. If that is not the cause then my next guess would be the O2 sensor.<P>If you are still using the original distributor or if the distributor is still hooked up to the ECM. If the O2 sensor picks up a rich condition and if it can't adjust for it then it will retard the timing. I've had this happen to me before on a TPI SBC that had injectors that were too large.<P>Put a timing light on it and rev. it up until it starts to back off and see if the timing retards.<P>Archie<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||falconhulk||09-18-2001||08:32 AM||||Archie he is not using computer advance.  He is using vacuum advance off the distributer.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Taijiguy||09-18-2001||09:04 AM||||This could be any number of things. If it were me, I'd check things out in this order:<P>Check to see that the secondaries are opening. Locate the rod that extends from the vacuum diaphragm to the control arm. Put a paperclip on the rod so it's up against the bottom of the diaphragm. Drive it, and get on it so the secondaries (should) open. Look to see if the paperclip has moved, and how much. If it hasn't moved, or moves only a little bit, you may have a ruptured diaphragm, or a leak. <BR>Check the power valve. Even a tiny little backfire can blow out a power valve. They make a tool for checking them, but at about 6 bucks a pop, they're cheap to replace. Also, while you're at it, make sure it's sized right. To do this, get a vacuum gauge, and connect it to manifold vacuum. Use a long enough hose so you can read it from inside the car. Drive the car around, and try to get an average for the vacuum. Often, it will be around 8 to 10 inches. Once you determine that number, you want to install a power valve that's rated about 2 or 3 inches below your average vacuum reading. Too high of a number, and it'll idle rich as hell, too low, and it'll never open.<BR>These are just the two things that come to mind offhand. <BR>A couple of other possible problems can be high or low fuel pressure, blocked vent tubes, high float setting (fuel level) worn float valves, leaking acelerator pump diaphragm. Timing is also a possibility as has been mentioned. <BR>I'm pretty good with Holleys, feel free to email me directly if you need more help. ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Formula||09-18-2001||10:49 AM||||Taiji..<P>I did everything you said before, and I had 7-8 lbs of vacuum.  when in neutral or park the engine revs up fine, no stuttering or anything, but in drive it starts to stutter at higher rpms.  I have the distributor vacuum coming off the primary intake vacuum now and that helped A LOT.  I still cant figure out why it needs to be 20 degs advanced to run right.  And the more I advance it the better it runs.  But, I tried turning the distributor (notch at a time) and it screws it all up and won't time at all (no start).  Its still faster then a new Mustang GT on the interstate.  its just the whole not making it to the powerband thats confusing me.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Taijiguy||09-18-2001||10:59 AM||||Formula:<BR>What size power valve do you have in it? With 7-8 inches, you should probably use a 5.5<BR>Check the jets too. If that motor is so radical, you may need larger jetting. If the motor bogs only when the secondaries are open, you might need a bigger metering block, or maybe you have an obstruction inside the metering block. Also, check the screen to the fuel inlet on the float bowl. There's a little catch screen in there, maybe it's blocked up, restricting fuel flow.||||reg||1
Z||000017||falconhulk||09-18-2001||11:08 AM||||Wait a minute!  That I just reread your posts.  edlebrock 390?  is that 390 cfm on a 350?  If so thats to low.  If you go vacuujm secondaries I would go with a 750cfm.  Most people will tell you not to go over 625 cfm but with vacuum secondaries it does not matter.  You dont have enough carb if a edlebrock 390 is 390 cfm.  For more in depth help go to <A HREF="http://thirdgen.org" TARGET=_blank>http://thirdgen.org</A> <BR>There is a whole section or carbed 350's.  But I really feel stepping up to a 750 cfm vacuum secondary would fix the problem.  I have a 750 cfm or a bored out 302 in my mustang with no bog.  I have a 725cfm quadrajet on my firebird - and it is stock.<p>[This message has been edited by falconhulk (edited 09-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||Formula||09-18-2001||12:23 PM||||falconhulk... its a 3.2 liter V6 thats supposed to be dynoed at 270 hp @7000 rpms.. I wish I would have used an edelbrock carb now instead of the holley   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">   <P>it still has the stock power valve.. I dont know what it is, its a new holley 390.  I'll have to look into the jetting too.  <P>Would bogging down when I give it a lot of gas be the secondaries?  ITS FAST at lower rpms but when the power is supposed to be huge it doesnt have anything.<p>[This message has been edited by Formula (edited 09-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||falconhulk||09-18-2001||12:24 PM||||OK I misunderstood the post "350 carb"  I thought you meant a V8 chevy 350!  Sorry bout that. It sounds to me like a secondary problem.  You said while sitting still and reving it is fine right?  Well when moving the engine is under load and the secondaries open and dump in fuel.  That sounds like your problem.  Its a very difficult problem to troubleshoot as those secondaries only open underload.  You might try another carb, thats the easiest way.  <p>[This message has been edited by falconhulk (edited 09-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||Taijiguy||09-18-2001||12:39 PM||||Stock 390 comes with a 6.5 power valve. That would probably be OK, it might just be a bit rich at idle. I don't know how you resolved the fuel pump situation, but you might look into insufficient fuel pressure, if you ran a bypass on the stock pump.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Formula||09-18-2001||01:32 PM||||I did what you told me to do and ran the return line back.  do you think if I found a used edelbrock 390 and put it on the car to test it I could figure out if its the secondaires?||||reg||1
Z||000022||Taijiguy||09-18-2001||04:39 PM||||You should probably pick up a fuel pressure gauge to determine what kind of pressure you have. I was amazed to find out when I hooked up mine (with that same fuel delivery arrangement) that I only had about 1 psi at the carb. That could very well be your problem. I ended up just installing an aftermarket electric fuel pump. Now I'm running a solid 6-7 psi.||||reg||3
Z||000023||Formula||09-18-2001||04:48 PM||||did you have to take the old fuel pump out of the tank to install the electric fuel pump?  or can you just unhook the in-tank one and run the electric one?<P><P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B><I>STROKE THIS!</FONT></FONT></B></I>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Flag.jpg">    <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Formulasiggy.jpg">    <BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B><I>FORMULA</FONT></FONT></B></I>||||reg||1
